{
  "prompt": "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The composition is a 3/4 overhead view at 40 degrees, centered to ensure all four tostadas are fully visible, with generous negative space. The depth of field is shallow, keeping the front-most tostada in tack-sharp focus while the secondary plate slightly behind melts into a soft, creamy bokeh. Lighting is soft, directional natural light from a window to the left, casting gentle, elongated shadows that emphasize the texture of the food. The setting is a rustic, weathered oak tabletop with visible grain. The tostadas are served on two matte, off-white ceramic plates. Each plate holds two corn tortillas, lightly toasted to a golden-brown with slight charring. Each tortilla is generously topped with a base of rustic, roughly mashed pinto beans seasoned with cumin, followed by a layer of bright, vibrantly wilted baby spinach, a mound of soft, fluffy yellow-white scrambled egg curds, a heap of fresh pico de gallo with ruby-red tomato and green onion specks, slices of pale-green creamy avocado, and a scatter of golden-brown toasted pepitas. Props include a small wooden spoon resting beside a plate and a scattering of fallen pepitas and a small piece of spinach on the wood surface. The scene is lived-in and authentic, featuring a stray crumb, a tiny splash of pico de gallo juice, and natural, uneven layering. No plastic sheen, no excessive styling, no artificial lighting. Warm, inviting golden-hour tones. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. Indistinguishable from professional editorial cookbook photography."
}
